Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172027 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172027 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172166 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172166 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172166 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172166 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172166 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172166 #edb/ref 1045 "Diagonalization of 2x2 Symmetric Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172166 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186172890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172166 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172166 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172166 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b43f46e4-16a6-5109-897b-7d61b6455c74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172613 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172613 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93602" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172613 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172613 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172613 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7e02b11-9226-5c5b-9fda-bf26ea5fa1e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172613 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172615 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186171315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172615 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94024" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172615 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172615 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186172615 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6397dc6a-e670-5a27-9059-a00e14d9ffee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172615 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186172882 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172882 #edb/ref 1061 30552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172882 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a2aa8190-261c-5faa-a399-c992ada54b4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172882 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172883 #edb/ref 1061 30553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172883 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af3ae02e-e0d1-5714-a823-36990c3416dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172883 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172884 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172884 #edb/ref 1061 30578 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172884 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "136b060b-1871-5958-8dc0-d7310998c040" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172884 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186172027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172890 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172890 #edb/ref 1061 31262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172890 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "337f89b5-9c83-5bda-ac19-7d80b551f82e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172890 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173154 #edb/ref 1064 "A square matrix $A$ is **orthogonally diagonalizable** if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Note that this is equivalent to the equation\n\n$$\nD = P^TAP.\n$$\n\nSo, to orthogonally diagonalize a matrix $A,$ we need to find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ that satisfies the above equations.\n\nA helpful theorem allows us to determine whether a real matrix is orthogonally diagonalizable. It states the following:\n\n*A matrix $A$ with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.*\n\nConsequently, *every* symmetric matrix is orthogonally diagonalizable!\n\nFor example, the symmetric matrix \n\n![](images/b3/b3970eb885720ce2aa4394918c6c5f9f343e26aa60039df219abf56398f096db.svg)\n\nhas the orthogonal diagonalization\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]}^{T}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{T}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is orthogonal, and $D$ is diagonal. As usual, the entries on the main diagonal of $D$ are the eigenvalues of $A.$ Also, the columns of $P$ are *orthonormal* eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalues of $A.$\n\nThe procedure of the orthogonal diagonalization of a $2 \\times 2$ real symmetric matrix $A$ is almost the same as the diagonalization of a matrix:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$.\n\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of  $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of *unit orthogonal* eigenvectors of $A.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nIn the case of symmetric matrices, the two eigenvectors we compute in step 2 will always be orthogonal, provided that they correspond to different eigenvalues. However, we must also ensure that we *normalize* those eigenvectors." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173154 #edb/ref 1065 5006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173154 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c729ab04-2eef-5b7c-a28e-3d0f55428c54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173154 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354836 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186354836 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354836 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1383bfc2-4360-5709-a0a8-f10b27226553" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354914 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186354914 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354914 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1cf62f53-4cca-53fd-9904-3006d9e5e4c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355048 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186355048 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355048 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2ca90f25-8a2a-5154-8abc-8f1caaac1737" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355364 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186355364 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355364 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4e874988-4b8c-5650-b515-a02bdcf8db24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355390 #edb/ref 1080 "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186355390 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186355390 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5180d0c3-657e-5b75-ac2b-25066a4ea962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1