 orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=8$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$ Computing $A-(-5)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2\\sqrt{13}}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{3\\sqrt{13}}{13}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186394881 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394881 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394881 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 1759218