c785a6c-6e3c-5d7b-a2af-c97237d81ba3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492002 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492002 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94049" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492002 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492002 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186492002 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "23817e7f-6a06-51ca-bb89-ae0fdb8c7004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492002 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186519616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186519624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186519642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186519655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186519661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1045 "Diagonalization of 2x2 Symmetric Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186519655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518979 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "64fc3270-931c-55b0-ab11-941f63cb1739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518993 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518993 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518993 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03bfb243-3f51-50bb-a40d-8bd7b816d5c9" #edb/ref 